    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Я сам ничего не понял.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I did not understand anything myself.' The '-l' ending on the verb meaning 'understood' places the action in the masculine past tense.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Катя, ну прости меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Katya, please forgive me.' Denis anchors the line on 'Katya', while 'me' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Откуда взялись все эти тараканы?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where did all these cockroaches come from?' The grammar packs 'where from' and 'cockroaches' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хотел как лучше, пойми.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I meant well, understand.' The meaning hinges on 'I' working together with 'understand' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Дело совсем не в тараканах.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is not about the cockroaches at all.' In Russian, the idea of 'business' flows naturally into 'about cockroaches' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Любовь — это не просто красивые слова.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Love is not just beautiful words.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'love' and finishing on 'words'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Любовь — это настоящее доверие.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Love is genuine trust.' Notice how Russian expresses 'love' and 'trust' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Катя, я тебе доверяю честно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Katya, I honestly trust you.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'Katya' and finishing on 'honestly'. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "А дело уже не в тебе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And it is no longer about you.' Katya anchors the line on 'and', while 'to you' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Я теперь сама сомневаюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Now I have doubts myself.' The grammar packs 'I' and 'I doubt' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Нам лучше пока не встречаться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is better for us not to date for now.' Notice the reflexive suffix on the verb meaning 'to date', turning the action back on the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Прости меня, Денис.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Forgive me, Denis.' The meaning hinges on 'forgive' working together with 'Denis' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Мы всех обязательно накажем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will definitely punish everyone.' In Russian, the idea of 'we' flows naturally into 'we will punish' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Это от отеля за неудобства.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is from the hotel for the inconvenience.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'this' and finishing on 'inconvenience'. Denis answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И вернуться назад уже невозможно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And it is already impossible to go back.' Denis brings out the contrast between 'and' and 'to return' in a few short words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
